<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arles</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arelate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arles is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> in Provence, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the head of the river's delta. As Roman Arelate it was a leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, with an amphitheatre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> that still stand, and an imperial residence in the 4th century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its archbishops were powerful, and it gave its name to the Kingdom of Arles, formed from the Burgundian realms in the 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arles.jpg" alt="Arles" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chensiyuan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_arles_france_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
